  </subsection>

 <subsection><title>Logarithmic Equations</title>
  <p> 
    Once again, let's consider this familiar equation:

<me>\sqrt[3]{x}=2</me>

Here, in order to solve for <m>x</m>, we would apply the inverse operation of a cube root 
(i.e. cube both sides):

<me>\left(\sqrt[3]{x}\right)^3=2^3</me>
<me>x=8</me>

Just like how a cube and cube root are both inverses of each other, 
the natural logarithm and natural exponential functions are also inverses of each other. 
In other words, we would use the natural logarithm to "cancel out" the natural exponential function, 
and likewise, we would use the natural exponential function to "cancel out" the natural logarithm. 
Consider the following logarithmic equation:

<me>\ln x=5</me>

To "cancel out" the natural logarithm, we must use the natural exponential function. 
We must be mindful of how we apply the natural exponential function, though. 
To correctly apply this step, we must raise both sides of the equation \textbf{as the power of <m>e</m>}:

<me>e^{\ln x}=e^5</me>
<me>x=e^5 \approx 148.413</me>
  </p>

  <aside>
    <p>
      <alert> Study tip: </alert>
    </p>
    <p>  
      A common misconception to try to \textit{multiply} both sides of the equation by <m>e</m> 
      in order to cancel out the logarithm. 
       Again, this is not correct, since while <m>e</m> \textit{does} have numerical value, 
       <m>\ln</m> does not, so <m>e \cdot \ln</m> is meaningless.
   </p>
  </aside>

